# Rustain relay — systemd unit template (Story 18.4c-b, FR159).
#
# Generated by `rustain relay serve --print-service-unit`. This file is the
# checked-in reference the generator reads via include_str!, so the two can
# never drift.
#
# LOAD-BEARING TOKENS (each has a Layer-1 content assertion in
# `cli::relay::serve`; a mutant removing one must turn a test RED):
#
#  * Type=simple — `relay serve` runs in the foreground and has NO detach path,
#    so systemd owns this process directly. ⚠ Unlike `rustain.service.template`,
#    there is no `--foreground` token to add here: the daemon's launcher re-execs
#    and setsid-detaches, and that is what its token defeats. This binary does
#    not, so copying the token would be cargo-culting.
#
#  * ExecStart names ALL THREE addresses. A one-address unit cannot start with
#    TLS on: the captive-portal probe must run in plain text on a DIFFERENT port
#    from the relay listener, and every rustain client built from this relay's
#    URL probes UDP 7842 for address discovery.
#
#  * Restart=on-failure + RestartSec + the StartLimit* pair. `relay serve`
#    returns non-zero when the relay terminates on its own, which is exactly the
#    case Restart=on-failure must catch; the crash-loop guard stops a relay that
#    cannot bind from spinning. ⚠ This is the INIT SYSTEM supervising the
#    process. rustain supervising the embedded server — health, reload,
#    restart-on-internal-failure — is FR159's other clause and defers with
#    `DF-18-4c-b-SUPERVISION`.
#
#  * DynamicUser=yes + LoadCredential — the relay runs as an unprivileged,
#    dynamically allocated user, and the PEM pair reaches it through systemd's
#    credential store: the operator's two PEM files are read once at start-up
#    `%d/relay.crt` and `%d/relay.key` (the credentials directory), so the key
#    file itself never has to be world-readable. Requires systemd v248+.
#
#  * KillSignal=SIGINT — the graceful stop path listens for SIGINT, so
#    `systemctl stop` drains the relay's connections inside the stop timeout
#    instead of killing it by default disposition. systemd does not apply
#    Restart=on-failure to a stop it requested itself, so this changes how the
#    relay stops, not whether it restarts.
#
#  * AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE — ports below 1024 for a user
#    that has nothing else.
#
#  * ⛔ NEGATIVE TWIN: the dev flag must NEVER appear in this file. Dev mode
#    serves plain HTTP on loopback; a service unit carrying it would silently
#    serve a plaintext relay on a host the operator believes is running TLS.
#
# Placeholders ({...}) are substituted by the single-pass
# `adapters::template::render_template`. ⛔ Never `str::replace`.
[Unit]
Description=Rustain relay ({hostname})
After=network.target
StartLimitIntervalSec=60
StartLimitBurst=5

[Service]
Type=simple
ExecStart={exe} relay serve --http-addr {http_addr} --https-addr {https_addr} --quic-addr {quic_addr} --hostname {hostname} --cert %d/relay.crt --key %d/relay.key
DynamicUser=yes
LoadCredential=relay.crt:{cert}
LoadCredential=relay.key:{key}
AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
KillSignal=SIGINT
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=2

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
